THE CORE WHY
The schema exists to enforce one distinction: gross_rent versus net_received.
Statements LEAD with the disbursed amount because that is the number an owner
watches day to day, and that is exactly how the email parser came to store net
in a column the tax code reads as gross. Every field description says which is
which, and the instruction states they "must never be reported as the same
value".

DESIGN DECISIONS
- No coercion. A model returning "$4,400.00" must fail loudly; a silently
  comma-stripped figure ends up in a tax return. Tested on gross_rent and
  net_received.
- Absent key rejected, null accepted. Null is a real answer ("not found"); a
  missing key is a malformed response. Tested specifically on other_outgoings,
  because absent-becomes-null there would make a statement where the agent paid
  a tradesperson appear to reconcile when it does not.
- Zero and null kept distinct: zero asserts the agent charged nothing, null
  means not stated.
- The instruction tells the model to report figures exactly as read even when
  they do not reconcile, and to note it. Forcing a balance would destroy the
  discrepancy the reconciliation exists to surface. A schema test asserts a
  non-reconciling extraction still parses.
- Fees requested GST-INCLUSIVE as printed: residential rent is input-taxed, so
  no GST credit is claimable and the inclusive figure is the deductible one.
- No server-side pdf-parse, for the DOMMatrix/Vercel bundler reason recorded in
  extract-loan-statement.ts.

